WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Skate or Die 2, the search for double trouble.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:27.760
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.660
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.160
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:40.580
When you think of Skate or Die, I think the part that stands out to me the most about the whole franchise is the title, right?

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.760
Skate or Die, very aggressive take for a skateboarding game.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.560
Nothing ever comes quite close to it.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.720
We could put a lot of things, right?

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.420
We could put like, or die, next to a lot of different actions, and they'd be great video games, right?

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.820
I think that Skate is probably like the perfect one, though.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.260
Perfect. Interesting.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.720
Okay. I have nothing to say beyond that.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.440
You wouldn't be interested in like, football or die?

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.880
Yeah, no, no, no.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:16.300
I also think that, a little off topic, but like, funny or die, which would be like, you know, probably the most popular version of this, is probably the most non sequitur.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.500
Yeah. There you go.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:26.100
Yeah. I think, if you're going for this like, 80s, I guess this isn't really 90s, but like that 80s, like, cool kid feel Skate or Die works.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.460
But like, if you want to go this more intense route, like fight or die.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.740
Yeah, that's just, too, it's kind of a different feeling.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.840
It's redundant. I don't know.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:54.800
Now, the other thing about Skate or Die, specifically in this Skate or Die 2, when you look up any information about this game, the first thing that you're most likely to hear somebody talk about is the little song on the title menu that sings out the title of the game, Skate or Die.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.840
You know, anything else like that where the first thing comes to your mind is the main menu?

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.880
Pictionary. Of course.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:15.100
The select screen for that Donkey Kong double feature cartridge.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.540
Take yourself out of the NES. Take yourself out of the NES for this one.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.940
I just meant like, you know, I guess in a weird way, I don't think it's the defining part of the game.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.480
But like Halo, you know, like the original Halo.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.360
That's a good version of that.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:51.440
But I'd say that the kind of in the same vein of like, oh, this is kind of embarrassing would be the PS2 remake of Spy Hunter had a new metal song called by Seether that I believe had a music video play before the game started for like an original song for the game.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.020
Which is not the only video game where that's happened.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:55.860
And that's crazy.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:04.180
The idea that at one point they were like, yeah, we should just have like, you know, bands play a song and we'll do like an FMV.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.880
Yeah. PsyOps, the Mindgate conspiracy had cold.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.580
I don't know what your example is, but this this goes even before the main menu.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.000
So now it's like, how much further can we push it?

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.120
But I just remembered this on a run the other day.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:33.280
I was just thinking about the old Neversoft animation with the like worm eye, the eye worm, like trying to escape the letters and then getting stabbed through the eye.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.460
It's like that was so violent.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.580
Yeah, it was like a it's like our Pixar.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.540
And what kind of name is that?

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.060
Neversoft? Soft. Always hard.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.340
You know what they mean.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.480
I do.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.820
But I also think it's a little clever for soft being software.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:55.620
Yeah, right, right. But no, I just I guess I'm so confused by that because I never once thought about it like that as a kid.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.900
I was never like, oh, that's gruesome.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.640
Or like, why is it called Neversoft?

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.900
And now that's the kind of shit I'm thinking about as an adult.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.820
I don't think I ever thought about that until right now.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:05.940
Like even Neversoft.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.280
I was just like, yeah, that's a word. I never thought about the two parts of it together.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.340
I mean, even if you take out the double entendre, it sounds cool.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:19.480
So, Joe, are you just somebody who like the second a company announces that they're a company?

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.620
You're like, yeah, that's their name.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:26.360
I think that it was more like I knew them, you know, from like Tony Hawk and stuff.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:32.320
But like, it's not like I'm they just kind of melded into my brain before I ever actually focused on it.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.260
I was just like, let's get to the game.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.700
Here's all this random text in the beginning.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:35.760
I'm not paying attention to.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.400
And by the time I like looked at it, it was already like a part of my subconscious.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.780
Do you do you have that same feeling for David Bunch, Eric Knopp and Michael Kasaka?

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.900
I don't know what you're saying.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.580
You do. You know those people.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:49.660
They made the game.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:53.180
They made which game?

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.500
This game. It's just like this game.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.720
Very like weirdly put.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:08.120
I guess this is more like an early electronic arts thing where they would actually like feature the developer names back when they were more of like an art collective that gave a shit.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:09.400
But yeah, could you imagine?

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:14.320
They're actually like they might as well put in this artist bio section of the manual, which is where Sean pulled those names.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.240
They might as well have also said like, no, it's literally on the title screen.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.140
Yeah, it's on the manual.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:21.480
I thought you were talking about the part in the manual.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.380
There's a whole like there's a picture of the three of them with their skateboards.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:29.380
It is like a whole like, you know, who they are and stuff like that.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.120
And I think it's all very interesting.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:38.600
But, you know, like it's also so different from the EA of today where there are, you know, what?

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:40.220
Thousands of employees.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.600
Yeah, I think that's a good guess.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.340
Imagine having them on their title screen.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:45.960
Yeah, this is like 10 employees.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.940
Well, you know, you ever watch the credits at the end?

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.460
Like, like the names.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.280
I mean, that's like an hour of my time.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.940
Right. But like read the name, Sean, like be like, I'm going to remember that guy.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.100
I mean, here and there.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.980
It's nice of you. Yeah.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:03.420
I feel like I just look for like who directed it.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.040
Well, I want to know who did the QA for the Korean release.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.040
But like just that guy.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.260
Nobody else.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:15.080
You're always just looking.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.400
You watch the whole entire credit roll to find it.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.080
I wait until localization.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:27.400
Yeah. Could you believe that we actually received listener mail for the first time in a long time about something like specific to a game?

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:28.740
And that was for this episode.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.900
We got listener mail about Skater Night 2.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:34.440
Okay. And it was not like an opinion on the game.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.980
It wasn't like, hey, you know, like I grew up in this game.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.020
I have a lot of nostalgia for it.

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.640
Instead, it was just like, don't forget to mention this.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.540
It was notes. And that fact.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.300
It was notes. Right, right, yeah.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:46.360
Like, make sure you mention this.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:47.260
So I put it in here.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.480
The skater that is on the cover of Skater Night 2.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:52.840
Oh, it's the guy.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:58.600
Yeah. Is Jeff Whitehead, who apparently makes like some of the darkest.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.160
Black metal. Yeah.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:05.320
Black metal music for a band called Leviathan.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.460
And I was like, all right, I don't know what else to do with that.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.520
Because black metal is not my jam.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:13.860
But I'll say it.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:21.800
I wonder if this guy that sent this letter to us is like also like a Wikipedia editor.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:23.740
Oh, because it's in there.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:25.020
It's in there now. Yeah.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.420
I think it's an important thing to know.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:36.240
It is definitely like an interesting, I'm not going to say coincidence, but like it's not like he's wearing a shirt that says Leviathan.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.240
I don't even know if Leviathan was a project at this point.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:43.180
Yeah, I don't know. So it's more like, hey, yeah, I'll do your skateboard thing here.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:45.640
And also like where is he skateboarding?

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:47.160
It's like a mall.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:48.700
Let me pull this up.

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:50.460
Well, I'm looking at the manual again.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.300
I don't know if I have the box art in front of me.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.740
I'm looking at the manual and it looks like he's like in a mall.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.920
Yeah. And I think that is a multi-tier mall.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.260
Maybe that's, you know, reference to the, yeah, that's reference to the level in the game where you're in a mall.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:07.900
No, I agree.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:09.420
Speaking of. That's pretty good.

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:11.300
Game starts in a weird way.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.560
Speaking of the game. Yeah.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.300
Starts in a really strange way.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.480
There's the opening cut scene.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:23.940
You, first off, you meet a lot of interesting people before you ever meet yourself.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:33.300
Right. So you've got these people that have a, they all have like a skater aesthetic or, you know, at least like a punk rock thing going on.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.000
And then you see, I guess, I don't know if they have a name or not.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:44.460
I'm just going to say you see the player character and it is like just the most boring, like white bread person.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.000
I've ever seen him. You know.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.360
Yeah. You project onto him.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:53.540
I think the weirdest one is like there's a Disney princess in here.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:02.420
